{
  "result": "At the edge of my town, there is a small museum devoted to sounds that have disappeared. It occupies an old bakery, so the air still seems faintly sweet when rain begins. Inside, visitors wear headphones and choose a drawer. One holds the rattle of a train crossing that was replaced by a bridge. Another contains the deliberate click of a rotary telephone, followed by the breath of someone deciding what to say.\n\nThe curator, Mara, collects recordings from people rather than archives. She says memory changes a sound, and that change is part of its history. A retired mechanic once imitated his father's workshop door so convincingly that a visitor turned around. A child contributed the noise her grandmother made while stirring soup, a soft scrape followed by a tiny knock against the pot.\n\nMy favorite drawer is labeled Summer Evenings. It begins with crickets and ends with the squeal of a screen door, but between them sits a long silence. The person who donated it said that, during those seconds, his brother was running across the yard to tell him a joke. He cannot remember the joke. He remembers waiting to laugh.\n\nI used to think preservation meant keeping things exactly as they were. Now I think it can mean making room for their absence. When the museum closes, Mara leaves one drawer slightly open. No recording plays. People still pause beside it, listening as if something might return. Sometimes, I stand there with them, grateful for quiet.",
